100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Recent advances in artificial intelligence and biomedical signal processing have significantly enhanced the ability to analyze physiological data for medical diagnosis and monitoring. Among various biological signals, human speech signals have emerged as a promising non-invasive biomarker for detecting neurological disorders. Speech production is a complex physiological process involving coordination between the respiratory system, vocal folds, articulatory muscles, and the central nervous system. Any impairment in neurological control may result in measurable alterations in speech characteristics.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Speech analysis provides an attractive solution for early disease detection because it is non-invasive, low-cost, and easy to implement. With the development of machine learning algorithms, researchers have been able to analyze complex speech features and detect subtle patterns that are not easily noticeable through traditional clinical evaluation.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Recent research indicates that acoustic features such as fundamental frequency \(F0\), jitter, shimmer, harmonic-to-noise ratio \(HNR\), and Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients \(MFCCs\) contain valuable information about the stability and quality of speech signals. These features can be extracted from voice recordings and used as input to machine learning models to classify individuals as healthy or potentially affected by neurological conditions.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E (Therefore, this project proposes the development of a machine learning-based system for analyzing speech signals to detect potential neurological disorders. The proposed system will record voice samples, extract relevant acoustic features, and use classification algorithms to estimate the probability of neurological abnormalities.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
